concept_apply("S22 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","Data / Domain Types",
    "eSewa/Khalti store wallet balances as DECIMAL(precise to the paisa), phone numbers as VARCHAR (to keep the leading 0 and '+977'), and timestamps as DATETIME. A fee table storing amounts as FLOAT would slowly drift by paisa across millions of rows — a real accounting bug.",
    "\"FLOAT is fine for money.\" FLOAT/DOUBLE are binary approximations: 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3, so totals drift. Use DECIMAL(p,s) for any currency or exact amount; reserve FLOAT for scientific measurements where tiny error is acceptable.",
    "A data type declares a column's domain — the values it may hold — enforcing correctness and controlling storage. Common MySQL types: INT, DECIMAL(p,s) for exact money, VARCHAR(n)/CHAR(n) for text, DATE/DATETIME, BOOLEAN. Money uses DECIMAL (not FLOAT); phone numbers use VARCHAR (not INT) to preserve leading zeros.",
    "data type / domain · INT · DECIMAL(p,s) · VARCHAR vs CHAR · DATE · money≠FLOAT · phone≠INT")

concept_understand("S22 · Concept 3 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","Specifying Constraints",
    "A constraint is a rule the DBMS enforces on every row, rejecting data that breaks it. The six you must know: PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, DEFAULT.",
    ["PRIMARY KEY = UNIQUE + NOT NULL, one per table (identifies each row).",
     "FOREIGN KEY links to another table's PK; optional ON DELETE CASCADE/RESTRICT.",
     "NOT NULL forbids blanks; UNIQUE forbids duplicates (but allows one NULL).",
     "CHECK validates a condition (marks 0–100); DEFAULT supplies a value if none given."],
    None,"A constraint is a bouncer at the door — bad data never gets in.",
    "~9 min. A student can't enroll in a course that doesn't exist → FK on Enrollment.course_id. Colour-code each constraint on the CREATE snippet.")
add_table_slide("S22 · Concept 3 · comparison","The six constraints — what each enforces (and PK vs UNIQUE)",
    ["Constraint","Rule it enforces","Duplicates?","NULLs?","College example"],
    [["PRIMARY KEY","uniquely identifies a row","no","no","Student.roll"],
     ["FOREIGN KEY","value must exist in parent table","depends","depends","Enrollment.course_id → Course"],
     ["NOT NULL","value must be present","yes","no","Student.name NOT NULL"],
     ["UNIQUE","no two rows share the value","no","one NULL ok","email VARCHAR(60) UNIQUE"],
     ["CHECK","value satisfies a condition","yes","yes","CHECK (marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100)"],
     ["DEFAULT","fills a value if none supplied","yes","-","semester INT DEFAULT 1"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.5,2.3,0.9,0.9,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="The classic exam line: PRIMARY KEY = UNIQUE + NOT NULL, and only one per table. NOT NULL forbids blanks but ALLOWS duplicates; UNIQUE forbids duplicates but allows one blank.")
concept_apply("S22 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","Specifying Constraints",
    "A college DB uses FOREIGN KEY (roll) REFERENCES Student(roll) so nobody can enroll a student who doesn't exist; email VARCHAR(60) UNIQUE so two accounts can't share an email; and CHECK (marks BETWEEN 0 AND 100) so a typo of 780 is rejected at the database, not just in the app.",
    "\"PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE are the same.\" Not quite: PRIMARY KEY = UNIQUE + NOT NULL and there is only ONE per table; a table can have MANY UNIQUE columns, and UNIQUE still allows a single NULL. PK identifies the row; UNIQUE just prevents duplicate values.",
    "Constraints are rules the DBMS enforces on every row: PRIMARY KEY (unique + not null, one per table), FOREIGN KEY (must match a parent row, with ON DELETE options), NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK (a condition), and DEFAULT. They move data-integrity rules into the database, so bad data is rejected regardless of which app inserts it.",
    "PRIMARY KEY · FOREIGN KEY · NOT NULL · UNIQUE · CHECK · DEFAULT · PK = UNIQUE + NOT NULL")

