# ============================================================ S24
add_divider("Session 24 · Lecture hour 3 (of 15)","Rename · Tuple Variables · String Operations",
    "When you join the Student table TO ITSELF to find classmates, both copies are called 'Student'. How does SQL tell them apart?",
    "OPENING HOOK [~5 min]. Agenda: AS (rename) → tuple variables (aliases & self-joins) → string ops (LIKE, wildcards, CONCAT). Pin the schema.")

concept_understand("S24 · Concept 1 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","The Rename Operation (AS)",
    "AS renames a column or table in the OUTPUT (or for the query's duration) so results are readable and expressions get a name. The underlying table is unchanged.",
    ["SELECT name AS student_name, marks AS score FROM Enrollment;",
     "AS on a computed column gives it a header: marks*1.0/100 AS fraction.",
     "The AS keyword is optional in MySQL (name student_name works) but clearer with it.",
     "It renames only the result — the stored column name never changes."],
    None,"AS = a nickname for output — the real column keeps its name.",
    "~7 min. A marksheet export should read 'Student Name', not 'name'. AS is the header-labelling tool.")
add_table_slide("S24 · Concept 1 · examples","AS in action — expression → readable output header",
    ["Query fragment","Output header","Why rename"],
    [["SELECT name AS \"Student Name\"","Student Name","human-readable report column"],
     ["marks AS score","score","clearer than 'marks' in context"],
     ["marks*1.0 AS percentage","percentage","computed column needs a name"],
     ["COUNT(*) AS total_students","total_students","aggregates have no natural name (S26)"],
     ["salary*12 AS annual_salary","annual_salary","label the derived value"],
     ["Student AS s","(table alias s)","short name for multi-table queries"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.9,1.9,2.2],fs=10.5,
    note="Rename columns for readable reports and to NAME computed/aggregate values (which otherwise get an ugly auto-name). Renaming a TABLE (Student AS s) leads straight into tuple variables.")
concept_apply("S24 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","The Rename Operation (AS)",
    "A college marksheet export must show headers like 'Student Name' and 'Percentage', not the raw column names 'name' and a formula. SELECT name AS 'Student Name', marks*1.0 AS 'Percentage' produces a report humans can read, without touching the stored schema.",
    "\"Renaming with AS changes the column in the table.\" It does not — AS only labels the result set for this query. The stored column name is unchanged; the next query still sees the original name.",
    "The rename operation (AS) gives a column, expression, or table a new name for the query's output or duration. It makes reports readable, names computed and aggregate columns, and provides short table aliases — without altering the stored schema. In MySQL the AS keyword is optional but recommended for clarity.",
    "rename · AS · column alias · expression alias · table alias · output only")

concept_understand("S24 · Concept 2 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","Tuple Variables (Table Aliases)",
    "A tuple variable is a short alias bound to a table (Student s). It shortens multi-table queries and is MANDATORY for a self-join, where a table is joined to itself and both copies need distinct names.",
    ["SELECT s.name, e.grade FROM Student s, Enrollment e WHERE s.roll = e.roll;",
     "Prefix columns with the alias (s.name) to say which table you mean.",
     "Self-join: FROM Student s1, Student s2 — two independent handles on one table.",
     "Without distinct aliases, a self-join is ambiguous and errors."],
    "s24_selfjoin.png","Aliases are handles — a self-join needs two handles on the same table.",
    "~9 min. Self-join 'students in the same program as Sita' with Student s1, Student s2. Make the two-copies idea visual.")
add_table_slide("S24 · Concept 2 · examples","Why tuple variables matter — with vs without aliases",
    ["Task","Query (MySQL)","Aliases needed?"],
    [["student + their grade","FROM Student s, Enrollment e WHERE s.roll=e.roll","optional, but tidy"],
     ["classmates (same program)","FROM Student s1, Student s2 WHERE s1.program=s2.program AND s1.roll<>s2.roll","MANDATORY (self-join)"],
     ["instructor + course taught","FROM Instructor i, Teaches t WHERE i.inst_id=t.inst_id","optional, tidy"],
     ["pairs of courses in same dept","FROM Course c1, Course c2 WHERE c1.dept=c2.dept AND c1.course_id<>c2.course_id","MANDATORY (self-join)"],
     ["disambiguate same column name","s.roll vs e.roll","needed when names clash"]],
    per_page=5,widths=[2.1,3.4,1.6],fs=10.5,
    note="For ordinary multi-table queries aliases are convenience; for a SELF-join they are mandatory — the two copies of the table must have different names to be referred to at all.")
concept_apply("S24 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","Tuple Variables (Table Aliases)",
    "To find everyone in Sita's program, you compare Student against itself: FROM Student s1, Student s2 WHERE s2.name='Sita' AND s1.program=s2.program AND s1.roll<>s2.roll. The two aliases s1 and s2 are the only way to hold two rows of the same table at once.",
    "\"Aliases are just cosmetic shortening.\" For most queries, yes — but for a self-join they are MANDATORY: without two distinct names, SQL cannot tell the two copies of the table apart and the query is ambiguous.",
    "A tuple variable (table alias) is a short name bound to a table for a query, e.g. Student s. It shortens multi-table queries and disambiguates columns (s.roll vs e.roll). It is mandatory for a self-join, where the same table appears twice and each copy needs its own name (s1, s2) to be referenced.",
    "tuple variable · table alias · self-join · disambiguation · s1/s2")

concept_understand("S24 · Concept 3 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","String Operations",
    "SQL searches and transforms text: LIKE with wildcards % (any sequence) and _ (exactly one character), CONCAT() for joining strings, and UPPER/LOWER/TRIM for case and whitespace.",
    ["SELECT name FROM Student WHERE name LIKE 'S%'; — names starting with S.",
     "email LIKE '%@pu.edu.np' — anything ending in that domain.",
     "_ matches exactly one character; % matches any run (including none).",
     "CONCAT(name,' (',program,')') builds display strings; use CONCAT in MySQL (not ||)."],
    None,"% = 'blah blah anything'; _ = exactly one Scrabble tile.",
    "~9 min. Ncell numbers → phone LIKE '98%'; Gmail users → email LIKE '%@gmail.com'. Note MySQL uses CONCAT, not ||.")
add_table_slide("S24 · Concept 3 · examples","LIKE patterns — pattern → meaning → sample match",
    ["Pattern (MySQL)","Meaning","Matches","Does NOT match"],
    [["LIKE 'S%'","starts with S","Sita, Sharma","Hari"],
     ["LIKE '%a'","ends with a","Sita, Anjali","Hari"],
     ["LIKE '%it%'","contains 'it'","Sita, Smith","Hari"],
     ["LIKE '_a%'","2nd letter is a","Hari, Rai","Sita"],
     ["LIKE '98%'","phone starts 98 (Ncell/Ntc)","9841234567","01-4412345"],
     ["LIKE '%@pu.edu.np'","college email domain","sita@pu.edu.np","x@gmail.com"],
     ["= 'Sita'","EXACT match only","Sita","sita, Sit"]],
    per_page=7,widths=[1.9,2.3,1.9,1.6],fs=10.5,
    note="% = any sequence (incl. empty); _ = exactly one character. LIKE does PATTERN matching; = does EXACT matching — the two are not interchangeable.")
concept_apply("S24 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","String Operations",
    "The search-as-you-type on Daraz and Hamrobazar is LIKE '%term%' under the hood (or full-text search for scale); a college can list all official emails with email LIKE '%@pu.edu.np'; a telecom finds its number range with phone LIKE '98%'.",
    "\"= and LIKE are the same.\" = is EXACT (name = 'Sita' matches only 'Sita'); LIKE does PATTERN matching with % and _. Also, LIKE 'Sita' with no wildcard behaves like = — the wildcards are what make LIKE powerful.",
    "String operations search and transform text: LIKE matches patterns using % (any sequence) and _ (exactly one character); CONCAT() joins strings (MySQL uses CONCAT, not ||); UPPER/LOWER/TRIM adjust case and whitespace. LIKE is pattern matching, distinct from = (exact match).",
    "LIKE · % (any) · _ (one) · CONCAT · UPPER/LOWER/TRIM · pattern ≠ exact")

