# ============================================================ S26
add_divider("Session 26 · Lecture hour 5 (of 15)","Aggregate Functions · GROUP BY · HAVING",
    "What's the AVERAGE salary in the IT department? How MANY students passed DBMS? You don't want 5,000 rows — you want one number.",
    "OPENING HOOK [~5 min]. Agenda: the five aggregates → GROUP BY (per-group) → HAVING (filter groups). Pin the schema.")

concept_understand("S26 · Concept 1 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","The Five Aggregate Functions",
    "An aggregate function collapses many rows into a single summary value. The five: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX. Over a whole table they return one row.",
    ["SELECT COUNT(*), AVG(marks), MAX(marks) FROM Enrollment WHERE course_id='IT220';",
     "COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values of col (S27).",
     "SUM/AVG work on numbers; MIN/MAX work on numbers, dates, and text.",
     "Aggregates ignore NULLs (except COUNT(*)) — a NULL mark is skipped by AVG."],
    None,"An aggregate boils many rows down to ONE number.",
    "~9 min. eSewa daily volume = SUM(amount). Preview the COUNT(col) vs COUNT(*) NULL subtlety for S27.")
add_table_slide("S26 · Concept 1 · examples","The five aggregates — function → question → result",
    ["Function","Answers the question","College example","Result (seed)"],
    [["COUNT(*)","how many rows?","students in IT220","4"],
     ["COUNT(grade)","how many non-NULL grades?","graded IT220 rows","3 (one NULL)"],
     ["SUM(marks)","total of a column?","total marks in IT220","259"],
     ["AVG(marks)","the average?","average IT220 marks","86.3 (NULLs skipped)"],
     ["MIN(salary)","the smallest?","lowest instructor salary","60000"],
     ["MAX(marks)","the largest?","top mark in IT220","95"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.6,2.3,2.1,1.5],fs=10.5,
    note="COUNT(*) counts every row; COUNT(col) skips NULLs; AVG/SUM/MIN/MAX also skip NULLs. This NULL behaviour is the bridge to S27.")
concept_apply("S26 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","The Five Aggregate Functions",
    "Every dashboard KPI is an aggregate: eSewa's 'total transactions today' is COUNT(*), 'total volume' is SUM(amount), 'average order value' on Daraz is AVG(total). One number summarising millions of rows.",
    "\"COUNT(column) counts all rows.\" No — COUNT(col) counts only rows where col is NOT NULL. COUNT(*) counts every row regardless. They differ exactly when the column has NULLs (e.g. ungraded enrollments).",
    "Aggregate functions summarise many rows into one value: COUNT (row/value count), SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX. Over a whole table they return a single row. All ignore NULLs except COUNT(*), which counts every row — so COUNT(*) and COUNT(col) differ when col contains NULLs.",
    "COUNT · SUM · AVG · MIN · MAX · COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) · aggregates skip NULLs")

concept_understand("S26 · Concept 2 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","GROUP BY (Per-Group Aggregates)",
    "GROUP BY splits rows into groups by a column's value and returns one aggregate row per group. Every non-aggregated column in SELECT must appear in GROUP BY.",
    ["SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM Instructor GROUP BY dept; — one row per dept.",
     "Rows collapse: all IT instructors become a single IT row with the group's AVG.",
     "Rule: SELECT may list only the grouping columns plus aggregates.",
     "GROUP BY program gives average marks per program (BIM vs BCA)."],
    "s26_groupby.png","GROUP BY collapses rows into one summary row per group.",
    "~9 min. Average marks per program. Stress the 'non-aggregated column must be in GROUP BY' rule (MySQL's ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY).")
add_table_slide("S26 · Concept 2 · examples","GROUP BY — group by what → one row per group",
    ["Query","Groups formed","Output (one row per group)"],
    [["GROUP BY dept (Instructor)","IT, MGT, ECO","dept + AVG(salary) each"],
     ["GROUP BY program (Student)","BIM, BCA","program + COUNT(*) each"],
     ["GROUP BY course_id (Enrollment)","per course","course_id + AVG(marks) each"],
     ["GROUP BY program, semester","each (program, sem) pair","one row per combination"],
     ["GROUP BY dept + COUNT(*)","per dept","dept + how many instructors"],
     ["no GROUP BY, just AVG(salary)","whole table = one group","a single overall average"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.7,1.9,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="Golden rule: every column in SELECT that is NOT inside an aggregate must appear in GROUP BY (MySQL enforces this under ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY). Grouping by two columns groups by their combination.")
concept_apply("S26 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","GROUP BY (Per-Group Aggregates)",
    "A revenue-by-category chart is SELECT category, SUM(amount) FROM sales GROUP BY category; 'average marks per program' is GROUP BY program; 'orders per city' is GROUP BY city. Any 'per X' metric on a dashboard is a GROUP BY X.",
    "\"With GROUP BY I can SELECT any column.\" No — a non-aggregated column in SELECT MUST be in the GROUP BY list. SELECT name, AVG(marks) … GROUP BY program is illegal (which name? the group has many). Group by it or aggregate it.",
    "GROUP BY partitions rows into groups sharing a value and returns one aggregate row per group (e.g. average salary per department). Any column in SELECT that is not inside an aggregate function must appear in GROUP BY; MySQL enforces this via ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY. Grouping by multiple columns groups by their combination.",
    "GROUP BY · one row per group · non-aggregated columns must be grouped · per-group aggregate")

concept_understand("S26 · Concept 3 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","HAVING (Filter Groups)",
    "HAVING filters GROUPS after aggregation, using aggregate conditions. WHERE filters rows BEFORE grouping; HAVING filters groups AFTER. Aggregates can appear in HAVING but never in WHERE.",
    ["SELECT course_id, COUNT(*) FROM Enrollment GROUP BY course_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 30;",
     "WHERE runs first (drops rows), then GROUP BY, then HAVING (drops groups).",
     "You cannot put COUNT(*)>30 in WHERE — aggregates don't exist yet at row-filter time.",
     "Use WHERE to pre-filter rows, HAVING to filter the resulting groups."],
    None,"WHERE = bouncer at the door (per person); HAVING = inspector of each seated table.",
    "~9 min. 'Courses with more than 30 students' = HAVING COUNT(*)>30. Show why the aggregate can't go in WHERE.")
add_table_slide("S26 · Concept 3 · comparison","WHERE vs HAVING — the classic confusion",
    ["Question","WHERE","HAVING"],
    [["Filters what?","individual rows","groups (after GROUP BY)"],
     ["Runs when?","before grouping","after grouping/aggregation"],
     ["Aggregates allowed?","no (COUNT(*) is illegal here)","yes (COUNT(*)>30 is fine)"],
     ["Example","WHERE marks >= 40","HAVING AVG(marks) >= 40"],
     ["Analogy","bouncer per guest","inspector per table of guests"],
     ["Can use both?","yes — WHERE then HAVING","WHERE marks IS NOT NULL … HAVING COUNT(*)>30"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.7,2.5,2.6],fs=10.5,
    note="Order of evaluation: FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY. Put a plain-column condition in WHERE (faster, pre-filter); put an aggregate condition in HAVING.")
concept_apply("S26 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","HAVING (Filter Groups)",
    "'Courses with more than 30 students' is GROUP BY course_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 30; 'departments whose average salary exceeds Rs 70,000' is GROUP BY dept HAVING AVG(salary) > 70000. Any 'groups where the summary passes a threshold' report needs HAVING.",
    "\"Use WHERE COUNT(*) > 30.\" Illegal — aggregates can't appear in WHERE (rows aren't grouped yet). The aggregate condition belongs in HAVING, which runs after GROUP BY. WHERE is only for per-row conditions.",
    "HAVING filters groups after aggregation using aggregate conditions (e.g. HAVING COUNT(*) > 30), whereas WHERE filters individual rows before grouping. Aggregates are allowed in HAVING but not in WHERE. Both can be used together: WHERE pre-filters rows, then HAVING filters the resulting groups.",
    "HAVING · filters groups · WHERE filters rows · aggregate in HAVING not WHERE · evaluation order")

add_solved_problem("S26 · Hands-on","Average marks per course, only busy courses",
    "On the college schema, show each course's average marks, but only for courses with more than 30 enrolled students, best average first.",
    ["Group the enrollments by course: GROUP BY course_id.",
     "Compute the per-group summaries: SELECT course_id, AVG(marks) AS avg_marks, COUNT(*) AS n.",
     "Keep only busy groups with an aggregate filter: HAVING COUNT(*) > 30 (NOT a WHERE).",
     "Optionally pre-filter ungraded rows first: WHERE marks IS NOT NULL (a per-row condition).",
     "Sort the surviving groups: ORDER BY avg_marks DESC."],
    "SELECT course_id, AVG(marks) AS avg_marks, COUNT(*) AS n FROM Enrollment WHERE marks IS NOT NULL GROUP BY course_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 30 ORDER BY avg_marks DESC;",
    "~6 min. Point at each clause and name its job — this query touches WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, and ORDER BY together.")
