# ============================================================ S27
add_divider("Session 27 · Lecture hour 6 (of 15)","NULL Values · Three-Valued Logic",
    "A student's grade is blank — they haven't been graded yet. Is grade = grade true? In SQL... not necessarily. Welcome to NULL.",
    "OPENING HOOK [~5 min]. Agenda: what NULL means → three-valued logic (IS NULL) → NULL in aggregates & arithmetic. Pin the schema.")

concept_understand("S27 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","What NULL Means",
    "NULL represents a missing, unknown, or not-applicable value. It is NOT zero and NOT an empty string — those are actual values; NULL is the absence of any value.",
    ["Enrollment.grade is NULL for a student who is mid-semester (not yet graded).",
     "Instructor.salary is NULL for an unpaid intern (no salary applies).",
     "0 means 'a value of zero'; '' means 'an empty text'; NULL means 'we don't know'.",
     "A column allows NULL unless declared NOT NULL (S22)."],
    None,"NULL = a sealed box: you can't say it equals anything, you don't know what's inside.",
    "~7 min. Contrast balance = 0 (real, broke) vs balance = NULL (unknown / account not created).")
add_table_slide("S27 · Concept 1 · examples","NULL vs 0 vs '' — three different things",
    ["Value","Means","Wallet example","Wrong if you…"],
    [["NULL","unknown / not applicable","account not created yet","treat it as 0 balance"],
     ["0","a real zero","balance is genuinely empty","treat it as unknown"],
     ["'' (empty string)","present but blank text","no nickname set","confuse with NULL name"],
     ["grade = NULL","not yet graded","mid-semester enrollment","count it as a fail (0)"],
     ["salary = NULL","no salary applies","unpaid intern instructor","include in AVG as 0"],
     ["marks = 0","genuinely scored zero","student sat and failed","hide from the class average"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.6,2.1,2.2,1.9],fs=10.5,
    note="Confusing NULL with 0 or '' is a real, expensive bug. A NULL balance ('account not created') must never be shown as Rs 0 ('you have no money').")
concept_apply("S27 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","What NULL Means",
    "A payment system must distinguish balance = 0 (a real account with no money) from balance = NULL (account not yet created). Showing a not-yet-created wallet as 'Rs 0' would tell a user they have an empty account that doesn't exist — a genuine bug that has shipped in real fintech products.",
    "\"NULL equals 0 or an empty string.\" No — 0 and '' are real values; NULL is the absence of a value. NULL + 5 is NULL (not 5); a NULL name is not the same as a blank name '' — the difference matters for logic and totals.",
    "NULL represents missing, unknown, or not-applicable data — it is not zero and not an empty string, which are actual values. A NULL means 'no value is stored here'. Distinguishing NULL from 0/'' is essential: e.g. a NULL balance (account not created) differs from a 0 balance (account with no money).",
    "NULL · unknown/missing/not-applicable · not 0 · not empty string")

concept_understand("S27 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","Three-Valued Logic (TRUE / FALSE / UNKNOWN)",
    "Because NULL is unknown, any comparison with NULL yields UNKNOWN (not TRUE or FALSE). SQL logic has three values; WHERE keeps only rows that are TRUE — UNKNOWN rows are dropped.",
    ["grade = NULL evaluates to UNKNOWN, so it returns NO rows — never use it.",
     "Use IS NULL / IS NOT NULL to test for missing values.",
     "AND/OR follow three-valued truth tables (TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN).",
     "NOT UNKNOWN is still UNKNOWN — negation doesn't rescue it."],
    "s27_three_valued.png","= NULL ✗ (always UNKNOWN) · IS NULL ✓ (the correct test).",
    "~9 min. Walk the AND/OR truth tables with UNKNOWN. Show WHERE grade=NULL returns nothing; IS NULL fixes it.")
add_table_slide("S27 · Concept 2 · examples","Testing for NULL — wrong vs right",
    ["Goal","Wrong (returns nothing / bug)","Right"],
    [["find ungraded enrollments","WHERE grade = NULL","WHERE grade IS NULL"],
     ["find graded enrollments","WHERE grade <> NULL","WHERE grade IS NOT NULL"],
     ["students missing an email","WHERE email = NULL","WHERE email IS NULL"],
     ["interns (no salary)","WHERE salary = NULL","WHERE salary IS NULL"],
     ["treat NULL marks as 0","AVG(marks)","AVG(COALESCE(marks,0))"],
     ["default a NULL grade text","grade","COALESCE(grade,'N/A')"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.3,2.4,1.9],fs=10.5,
    note="= NULL and <> NULL always yield UNKNOWN, so they match no rows. Use IS NULL / IS NOT NULL. COALESCE(x, fallback) substitutes a value when x is NULL.")
concept_apply("S27 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","Three-Valued Logic",
    "A report of 'students with no email on file' must use WHERE email IS NULL. A developer who writes WHERE email = NULL gets zero rows and wrongly concludes 'everyone has an email' — a silent, dangerous bug caused by three-valued logic.",
    "\"= NULL finds the blanks.\" It never does — = NULL is UNKNOWN for every row, so it returns nothing. Always use IS NULL / IS NOT NULL. Likewise NOT IN with a NULL in the list can return no rows (a subtle S28 trap).",
    "In SQL, any comparison involving NULL yields UNKNOWN, giving three-valued logic (TRUE/FALSE/UNKNOWN). WHERE keeps only TRUE rows, so = NULL (always UNKNOWN) returns nothing — you must use IS NULL / IS NOT NULL. Logical operators follow three-valued truth tables (e.g. TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN).",
    "three-valued logic · TRUE/FALSE/UNKNOWN · IS NULL / IS NOT NULL · = NULL is always UNKNOWN")

concept_understand("S27 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","NULL in Aggregates & Arithmetic",
    "Arithmetic with NULL gives NULL (100 + NULL = NULL). Aggregates ignore NULLs — except COUNT(*). This can silently change results, so decide deliberately whether a NULL should be dropped or treated as a value.",
    ["SELECT 100 + NULL; returns NULL — one NULL poisons an arithmetic expression.",
     "AVG(marks) skips NULL marks entirely (they don't count in numerator or denominator).",
     "COUNT(*) counts NULL rows; COUNT(col) and SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX ignore them.",
     "Use COALESCE(marks,0) if an ungraded student SHOULD count as 0."],
    None,"One NULL in a sum poisons it to NULL; aggregates quietly skip NULLs instead.",
    "~8 min. Is a 'not yet graded' student a 0 in the average, or excluded? AVG drops them silently — COALESCE makes them count.")
add_table_slide("S27 · Concept 3 · examples","How NULL behaves in maths and aggregates",
    ["Expression / aggregate","Result with a NULL present","Note"],
    [["100 + NULL","NULL","any arithmetic with NULL → NULL"],
     ["marks * 1.0 (marks NULL)","NULL","the whole expression is NULL"],
     ["AVG(marks) over {88, NULL, 90}","89 (avg of 88 & 90)","NULL row dropped entirely"],
     ["COUNT(*) over 3 rows (1 NULL)","3","counts the NULL row"],
     ["COUNT(marks) over 3 rows (1 NULL)","2","skips the NULL"],
     ["SUM(marks) with a NULL","sum of non-NULLs","NULL contributes nothing"],
     ["AVG(COALESCE(marks,0))","includes 0 for the NULL","makes NULL count as 0"]],
    per_page=7,widths=[2.6,2.2,1.9],fs=10.5,
    note="Aggregates silently drop NULLs, which can quietly change an average. Decide on purpose: exclude (default) or count-as-zero (COALESCE). COUNT(*) is the one aggregate that counts NULL rows.")
concept_apply("S27 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","NULL in Aggregates & Arithmetic",
    "Averaging class marks where some students aren't yet graded: AVG(marks) silently drops the ungraded ones, giving the average of those graded. If policy says ungraded = 0, you must write AVG(COALESCE(marks,0)) — otherwise the reported average is misleadingly high.",
    "\"Aggregates include NULL rows as zero.\" No — SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX ignore NULLs entirely (they are not treated as 0). Only COUNT(*) counts NULL rows. If you want NULL to count as 0, wrap it in COALESCE.",
    "Arithmetic with NULL yields NULL (100 + NULL = NULL). Aggregates ignore NULLs — SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX and COUNT(col) skip them, while COUNT(*) counts every row. This can silently alter results, so use COALESCE(x, fallback) when a NULL should be treated as a concrete value (e.g. 0).",
    "arithmetic with NULL → NULL · aggregates skip NULLs · COUNT(*) counts all · COALESCE")

add_activity("S27 — Should ungraded count as 0?  ·  ~5 min",
    ["In pairs (2 min): a student is 'not yet graded' — should they count as 0 in the class average? Argue it.",
     "Write both queries: AVG(marks) vs AVG(COALESCE(marks,0)).",
     "Share aloud (3 min); discuss how NULL vs 0 changes the reported average.",
     "Close: the DB can't decide policy — you must choose to exclude or count-as-zero."],
    "Both are defensible: excluding (AVG(marks)) reflects only assessed work; counting-as-0 (COALESCE) penalises the ungraded. The lesson is that NULL forces an explicit policy choice.",
    "ACTIVITY [~5 min].")
