concept_apply("S31 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","UPDATE",
    "'Edit profile', 'change delivery address', 'apply a 10% salary revision' are UPDATEs. A bank raising IT-department salaries runs UPDATE Instructor SET salary=salary*1.10 WHERE dept='IT'; the WHERE is what stops every employee in the company getting the raise.",
    "\"UPDATE Enrollment SET grade='A';\" with no WHERE gives EVERY student an A. The fix: always test your WHERE with a SELECT first, and in production wrap changes in a transaction so you can ROLLBACK (Unit 6).",
    "UPDATE modifies existing rows: SET assigns new (possibly computed) values, and WHERE selects which rows change. Omitting WHERE updates every row — a common catastrophe — so always preview the affected rows with a matching SELECT first, and use transactions in production.",
    "UPDATE · SET · WHERE scopes it · missing WHERE = all rows · SELECT-first habit")

concept_understand("S31 · Concept 3 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","DELETE",
    "DELETE removes rows (a DML operation on data). WHERE decides which rows; no WHERE deletes them all. DELETE differs from DROP (DDL), which removes the whole table structure.",
    ["DELETE FROM Enrollment WHERE sem='2024F'; — removes only that semester's rows.",
     "DELETE FROM Enrollment; — removes ALL rows but keeps the table.",
     "Dropping a course may trigger FK behaviour: ON DELETE CASCADE vs RESTRICT (S22).",
     "DELETE removes rows; DROP removes the table itself; TRUNCATE fast-empties it."],
    None,"DELETE = erase lines in a notebook; DROP = throw out the whole notebook.",
    "~8 min. Dropping a course → FK ON DELETE CASCADE vs RESTRICT. Contrast DELETE (DML) vs DROP (DDL).")
add_table_slide("S31 · Concept 3 · comparison","DELETE vs DROP vs TRUNCATE — what disappears",
    ["Command","Removes","Keeps","Language"],
    [["DELETE FROM t WHERE …","matching rows","table + other rows","DML"],
     ["DELETE FROM t","all rows","the empty table","DML"],
     ["TRUNCATE TABLE t","all rows (fast, no per-row log)","the empty table","DDL"],
     ["DROP TABLE t","the whole table + all data","nothing","DDL"],
     ["ON DELETE CASCADE","child rows too, automatically","referential integrity","constraint (S22)"],
     ["ON DELETE RESTRICT","blocks delete if children exist","the parent row","constraint (S22)"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.1,2.3,2.0,1.1],fs=10.5,
    note="DELETE (DML) removes rows and can be rolled back in a transaction; DROP (DDL) removes the table. FK ON DELETE rules decide what happens to child rows when a parent is deleted.")
concept_apply("S31 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","DELETE",
    "'Cancel order', 'remove item from cart', 'clear old logs' are DELETEs — usually soft-deletes (a status flag) in production so nothing is truly lost. Purging last year's enrollments is DELETE FROM Enrollment WHERE sem='2024F'; the WHERE keeps this year's data intact.",
    "\"DELETE and DROP are the same.\" No — DELETE (DML) removes ROWS and keeps the table (and can be rolled back in a transaction); DROP (DDL) removes the entire TABLE and its structure. Confusing them can destroy a schema, not just data.",
    "DELETE removes rows from a table (DML); WHERE scopes which rows, and no WHERE deletes all rows while keeping the table. It differs from DROP (DDL), which removes the whole table, and TRUNCATE, which fast-empties it. Foreign-key ON DELETE CASCADE/RESTRICT rules govern child rows.",
    "DELETE (rows, DML) · DROP (table, DDL) · TRUNCATE · ON DELETE CASCADE/RESTRICT · soft-delete")

