# ============================================================ S34
add_divider("Session 34 · Lecture hour 13 (of 15)","Basic Concepts of Stored Procedures",
    "What if a 20-line enrollment routine — check seats, insert, update the count — could be stored IN the database and run with one call: CALL enroll(101,'IT220')?",
    "OPENING HOOK [~5 min]. Agenda: what a stored procedure is → parameters & control flow → why use them. Pin the schema.")

concept_understand("S34 · Concept 1 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","What a Stored Procedure Is",
    "A stored procedure is a named block of SQL plus procedural logic, stored in the database and executed on the server with CALL. Unlike a view, it can run many statements, take parameters, and modify data.",
    ["CREATE PROCEDURE enroll(IN r INT, IN c VARCHAR(10)) BEGIN INSERT INTO Enrollment(roll,course_id) VALUES(r,c); END;",
     "Invoke it with CALL enroll(101,'IT220'); — one call runs the whole block.",
     "It lives on the server, so the logic is central and reused by every app.",
     "A view is a virtual TABLE; a procedure is executable LOGIC."],
    "s34_procedure.png","A stored procedure = a saved recipe the kitchen runs on order.",
    "~9 min. Show the CREATE PROCEDURE shape and CALL. Contrast sharply with a view (data vs logic).")
add_table_slide("S34 · Concept 1 · comparison","Stored procedure vs view — logic vs virtual table",
    ["Aspect","View","Stored procedure"],
    [["What it is","a named query (virtual table)","a named block of logic"],
     ["Statements","one SELECT","many (INSERT/UPDATE/IF/loops)"],
     ["Parameters","no","yes (IN/OUT/INOUT)"],
     ["Can modify data?","only if updatable (S32)","yes, directly"],
     ["Invoked with","SELECT * FROM view","CALL proc(args)"],
     ["Best for","reuse a query / security","reuse multi-step business logic"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.7,2.4,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="A view packages a QUERY; a procedure packages LOGIC (multiple statements, parameters, control flow). Reaching for a procedure signals a multi-step operation, not just a saved SELECT.")
concept_apply("S34 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","What a Stored Procedure Is",
    "An enrollment routine — check seats remain, insert the enrollment, decrement the seat count — is stored once as a procedure and run with CALL enroll(101,'IT220'). Every app (web, mobile, admin) calls the same server-side logic, so the rules can't drift between clients.",
    "\"A procedure is just a saved SELECT (a view).\" No — a view is a virtual table (one query, no parameters, read-oriented). A procedure runs multi-statement procedural logic, accepts parameters, and can modify data. Different tools for different jobs.",
    "A stored procedure is a named block of SQL and procedural logic stored in the database and executed on the server via CALL. Unlike a view (a virtual table from one query), it can contain many statements, accept parameters, use control flow, and modify data — packaging reusable multi-step logic centrally.",
    "stored procedure · CREATE PROCEDURE · CALL · server-side logic · vs view (data)")

concept_understand("S34 · Concept 2 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","Parameters & Basic Control Flow",
    "Procedures take parameters — IN (input), OUT (return a value), INOUT (both) — and can use variables, IF, and loops. This makes them small programs that run inside the database.",
    ["IN passes a value in; OUT returns a value to the caller; INOUT does both.",
     "CREATE PROCEDURE transfer(IN frm INT, IN to INT, IN amt DECIMAL(10,2)).",
     "Control flow: DECLARE variables, IF … THEN, WHILE loops.",
     "Kept at concept level here — the point is 'procedures can hold logic', not deep coding."],
    None,"IN = ingredients you hand in; OUT = the dish handed back.",
    "~8 min. Sketch a transfer(from,to,amount) for an eSewa-style wallet move. Keep it conceptual.")
add_table_slide("S34 · Concept 2 · scaffolding","Procedure parameters & logic — what each part does",
    ["Element","Role","Wallet-transfer example"],
    [["IN param","value passed into the procedure","frm, to, amt"],
     ["OUT param","value returned to the caller","OUT new_balance DECIMAL(10,2)"],
     ["INOUT param","passed in and updated","INOUT retries INT"],
     ["variable (DECLARE)","local working value","DECLARE bal DECIMAL(10,2)"],
     ["IF … THEN","conditional logic","IF bal < amt THEN reject"],
     ["the two writes","debit + credit together","UPDATE sender; UPDATE receiver"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.8,2.5,2.3],fs=10.5,
    note="Parameters make a procedure reusable with different inputs; IN/OUT/INOUT control data direction. Control flow (IF, loops) lets the procedure make decisions the server-side — a small program next to the data.")
concept_apply("S34 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","Parameters & Basic Control Flow",
    "An eSewa-style transfer(from, to, amount) procedure checks the sender's balance (IF), debits one wallet and credits the other, and can return the new balance via an OUT parameter. Parameters let the one procedure serve every transfer, whatever the accounts and amount.",
    "\"Parameters are just for input.\" Not only — IN feeds values in, but OUT returns results to the caller and INOUT does both. A transfer procedure can return the new balance through an OUT parameter rather than requiring a second query.",
    "Stored procedures take parameters — IN (input), OUT (output back to the caller), INOUT (both) — and use variables, IF conditions, and loops. This lets a procedure act as a small program beside the data: e.g. a transfer(from,to,amount) that validates the balance and performs the debit and credit.",
    "IN / OUT / INOUT parameters · variables · IF · loops · reusable server-side logic")

concept_understand("S34 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","Why Use Stored Procedures",
    "Procedures give reuse, performance (fewer client-server round-trips), centralized business logic (one place, not copied in every app), and security (grant CALL without granting table access).",
    ["Reuse: write the enrollment logic once, call it from web, mobile, and admin.",
     "Performance: one CALL runs many statements server-side (one round-trip, not ten).",
     "Consistency/security: logic lives in the DB, and you can GRANT CALL without table rights.",
     "Debit+credit in one procedure keeps them together (bridges to transactions, Unit 6)."],
    None,"A procedure = the recipe the kitchen keeps, so every order comes out the same.",
    "~9 min. Bank fund-transfer as one procedure so debit+credit always run together. Forward-link to Unit 6 transactions.")
add_table_slide("S34 · Concept 3 · payoff","Why procedures — benefit → concrete payoff (Nepal / IT)",
    ["Benefit","What it gives","Example"],
    [["Reuse","write logic once, call everywhere","enroll() called by web + app + admin"],
     ["Performance","fewer client-server round-trips","one CALL vs 10 separate queries"],
     ["Centralized logic","rules live in one place","fee calculation can't drift between apps"],
     ["Security","GRANT CALL, not table access","staff can enroll without raw table rights"],
     ["Consistency","related steps run together","debit + credit in one transfer()"],
     ["Maintainability","fix the rule in one spot","change the seat-limit check once"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[1.8,2.4,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="Banks and fintechs push critical logic (transfers, interest calc) into procedures for consistency, security, and speed. The debit+credit-together point leads straight into transactions (Unit 6).")
concept_apply("S34 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","Why Use Stored Procedures",
    "Banks and fintechs put fund transfers and interest calculations in stored procedures so the logic is identical across every channel, runs fast in one round-trip, and can be granted (CALL) to staff without exposing the underlying tables. One definition, consistently applied.",
    "\"Procedures are outdated — put all logic in the app.\" For critical, shared, data-close logic (transfers, audits) procedures give consistency, security, and fewer round-trips that app-only logic can't match. Many systems use both: app logic for UI, procedures for critical data operations.",
    "Stored procedures offer reuse (write once, call from every app), performance (one server round-trip runs many statements), centralized business logic (rules can't drift between clients), and security (GRANT CALL without table access). This makes them ideal for critical shared operations like fund transfers — which also motivates transactions (Unit 6).",
    "reuse · performance (round-trips) · centralized logic · security (GRANT CALL) · consistency")

