# ============================================================ S35
add_divider("Session 35 · Lecture hour 14 (of 15)","DML Triggers · Indexing",
    "Every time a student is graded, the system should auto-log it — without the app remembering to. And when 'find roll 101' scans a million rows... why is it instant? Triggers and indexes.",
    "OPENING HOOK [~5 min]. Agenda: DML triggers → OLD/NEW & use cases → indexing → when indexes help/hurt. Pin the schema.")

concept_understand("S35 · Concept 1 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET]","DML Triggers",
    "A trigger is code that fires AUTOMATICALLY on an INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE — BEFORE or AFTER the event. You never call a trigger; the data-modification event fires it.",
    ["CREATE TRIGGER log_grade AFTER UPDATE ON Enrollment FOR EACH ROW INSERT INTO GradeAudit(...) VALUES(OLD.grade,NEW.grade,NOW());",
     "BEFORE triggers can validate/adjust a row; AFTER triggers react (e.g. audit).",
     "FOR EACH ROW runs the trigger once per affected row.",
     "Triggers enforce rules and side-effects the app can't forget to run."],
    "s35_trigger.png","A trigger fires by itself on the event — you never CALL it.",
    "~9 min. Auto-write to a GradeAudit table whenever a marks row changes → exam-integrity trail.")
add_table_slide("S35 · Concept 1 · examples","Trigger uses — event → automatic action",
    ["Trigger fires on","Timing","Automatic action"],
    [["UPDATE of Enrollment.marks","AFTER","write old+new to GradeAudit (audit trail)"],
     ["INSERT into Enrollment","BEFORE","reject if marks > 100 (defence-in-depth)"],
     ["DELETE from Student","AFTER","archive the deleted row"],
     ["UPDATE of wallet balance","AFTER","log the change for fraud analysis"],
     ["INSERT into orders","AFTER","decrement stock automatically"],
     ["UPDATE of salary","BEFORE","block a raise above a policy cap"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.4,1.3,3.0],fs=10.5,
    note="Triggers run side-effects the application must never forget — audits, derived-total maintenance, extra validation. BEFORE can veto/adjust the change; AFTER reacts once it has happened.")
concept_apply("S35 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","DML Triggers",
    "Exam-integrity systems use an AFTER UPDATE trigger on the marks column to write every change (old value, new value, timestamp, user) to a GradeAudit table — so any tampering leaves a trail, automatically, whether the change came from the app, an admin script, or raw SQL.",
    "\"You have to call a trigger like a procedure.\" No — a trigger fires by ITSELF on its INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE event; there is no CALL. That's its power (nothing can bypass it) and its danger (hidden side-effects if overused).",
    "A DML trigger is code that fires automatically on an INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE, either BEFORE (validate/adjust) or AFTER (react) the event, running FOR EACH ROW affected. It is never called explicitly — the event fires it — making it ideal for audit trails, enforcing rules, and maintaining derived data regardless of which client made the change.",
    "trigger · BEFORE/AFTER · INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE · fires automatically · FOR EACH ROW · audit")

concept_understand("S35 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","OLD vs NEW & Trigger Use Cases",
    "Inside a row-level trigger, OLD refers to the row's values before the change and NEW to the values after. Comparing them powers auditing, complex rule enforcement, and auto-maintaining derived totals.",
    ["UPDATE: OLD.grade is the previous grade, NEW.grade the incoming one.",
     "INSERT: only NEW exists; DELETE: only OLD exists.",
     "Audit: log OLD.grade → NEW.grade with NOW() on every marks change.",
     "Rule: a BEFORE trigger blocking marks > 100 backs up the CHECK from S22 (defence-in-depth)."],
    None,"OLD = the row before · NEW = the row after — compare them to react.",
    "~8 min. A trigger blocking marks > 100 as defence-in-depth alongside the S22 CHECK. Note OLD/NEW availability per event.")
add_table_slide("S35 · Concept 2 · scaffolding","OLD vs NEW by event, and what you'd do",
    ["Event","OLD available?","NEW available?","Typical use"],
    [["INSERT","no","yes","validate/adjust NEW before storing"],
     ["UPDATE","yes","yes","audit OLD→NEW; block bad changes"],
     ["DELETE","yes","no","archive OLD before it's gone"],
     ["BEFORE UPDATE marks>100","yes","yes","reject NEW.marks>100 (with S22 CHECK)"],
     ["AFTER UPDATE grade","yes","yes","INSERT (OLD.grade,NEW.grade,NOW()) to audit"],
     ["AFTER INSERT order","no","yes","decrement stock by NEW.qty"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.2,1.3,1.3,2.4],fs=10.5,
    note="OLD/NEW availability follows the event: INSERT has only NEW, DELETE only OLD, UPDATE has both. Comparing OLD and NEW is how a trigger audits changes and enforces cross-value rules.")
concept_apply("S35 · Concept 2 · [THEORY]","OLD vs NEW & Use Cases",
    "A marks-audit trigger stores OLD.marks, NEW.marks, and NOW() every time a grade changes, giving investigators a complete before/after history. A BEFORE UPDATE trigger that rejects NEW.marks > 100 adds a second line of defence behind the S22 CHECK constraint.",
    "\"Triggers can do everything, so put all logic in them.\" Over-using triggers creates hidden, hard-to-debug side-effects ('why did this row change?'). Use them for audits, integrity, and derived-data upkeep — not as a dumping ground for business logic better kept visible in procedures/app code.",
    "In a row-level trigger, OLD holds the pre-change values and NEW the post-change values; INSERT exposes only NEW, DELETE only OLD, UPDATE both. Comparing them enables auditing (log OLD→NEW), enforcing cross-value rules (block NEW.marks>100 as defence-in-depth with the CHECK), and auto-maintaining derived totals.",
    "OLD (before) · NEW (after) · availability per event · audit & rule enforcement · defence-in-depth")

concept_understand("S35 · Concept 3 · [THEORY] [SNIPPET] [HANDS-ON]","Indexing (CREATE INDEX)",
    "An index is an auxiliary structure (usually a B-tree) that lets the DBMS find rows by a column's value quickly, instead of scanning the whole table. It speeds up lookups, joins, and sorts on that column.",
    ["CREATE INDEX idx_student_program ON Student(program);",
     "A B-tree index lets a lookup jump to the row in a few hops, not a full scan.",
     "Indexes help WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY on the indexed column.",
     "The primary key is automatically indexed; you add indexes for other hot columns."],
    "s35_index.png","An index = the index at the back of a textbook — jump to the page, don't read every one.",
    "~9 min. CREATE INDEX on Student(program). Show table-scan vs index-lookup speed. HANDS-ON: EXPLAIN a query.")
add_table_slide("S35 · Concept 3 · examples","Table scan vs index lookup — the speed difference",
    ["Query","Without index","With index on the column"],
    [["WHERE roll = 101","scan every row","jump straight to it (B-tree)"],
     ["WHERE program='BIM'","scan whole table","seek matching rows fast"],
     ["JOIN … ON s.roll=e.roll","slow row-by-row match","fast indexed lookup per row"],
     ["ORDER BY marks","sort all rows each time","read in index order (pre-sorted)"],
     ["transactions WHERE user_id=…","scan millions of rows","index makes history load instant"],
     ["Khalti history page","slow, times out","idx on (user_id) → fast"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.6,2.2,2.5],fs=10.5,
    note="An index turns a full table scan (read every row) into a targeted lookup (a few B-tree hops). This is the #1 reason a slow app page becomes fast — index the columns you filter, join, and sort on.")
concept_apply("S35 · Concept 3 · [THEORY]","Indexing (CREATE INDEX)",
    "Khalti's transaction-history page filters by user_id over millions of rows; an index on transactions(user_id) turns a full-table scan into an instant lookup, so the page loads in milliseconds. Correct indexing is the single most common fix that turns a slow screen fast.",
    "\"An index stores a separate copy of the data.\" It stores an ordered structure of key values plus pointers to rows — not a full duplicate. Also, an index doesn't change query RESULTS, only their SPEED; a query returns the same rows with or without it.",
    "An index is an auxiliary (usually B-tree) structure that lets the DBMS locate rows by a column's value in a few hops instead of scanning the whole table, accelerating WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY on that column. CREATE INDEX name ON table(col). The primary key is indexed automatically; other frequently queried columns are indexed deliberately.",
    "index · B-tree · CREATE INDEX · table scan vs lookup · speeds WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY")

concept_understand("S35 · Concept 4 · [THEORY]","When Indexes Help — and Hurt",
    "Indexes speed reads but slow writes (every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must also maintain the index) and use extra storage. Index selectively: the columns you frequently filter, join, or sort on — not every column.",
    ["Help: frequent WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY on that column; high-selectivity columns.",
     "Hurt: every write must update the index too, so many indexes slow inserts/updates.",
     "Cost: storage space; unused indexes are pure overhead.",
     "Rule: index the read-hot columns; don't index everything 'just in case'."],
    None,"Every index you add is another book you must re-sort on every write.",
    "~8 min. Which Enrollment column to index (roll? course_id?) and why. Bust 'index every column'.")
add_table_slide("S35 · Concept 4 · payoff","Index this? — decision guide",
    ["Column / situation","Index it?","Why"],
    [["Enrollment.roll (joined often)","yes","speeds the student-grade join"],
     ["Student.program (filtered often)","yes","fast WHERE program='BIM'"],
     ["transactions.user_id (history page)","yes","turns a scan into a lookup"],
     ["a rarely queried notes column","no","overhead with no read benefit"],
     ["a column with 2 values (gender)","usually no","low selectivity — scan is as fast"],
     ["a write-heavy log's every column","no","each index slows every INSERT"]],
    per_page=6,widths=[2.6,1.1,2.6],fs=10.5,
    note="Index the columns you READ by (filter/join/sort), especially high-selectivity ones. Over-indexing slows writes and wastes storage — the classic 'index everything' mistake makes a write-heavy system slower, not faster.")
concept_apply("S35 · Concept 4 · [THEORY]","When Indexes Help — and Hurt",
    "On the Enrollment table, indexing roll (used in almost every join to Student) and course_id (used to filter by course) pays off hugely; indexing rarely-queried columns just slows every grade entry. A write-heavy audit log, by contrast, is often left lightly indexed so inserts stay fast.",
    "\"Index every column for maximum speed.\" Over-indexing slows every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE (each index must be maintained) and wastes storage. Index the columns you actually filter, join, or sort on — selectivity and read-frequency decide, not 'just in case'.",
    "Indexes speed reads (WHERE/JOIN/ORDER BY) but slow writes, since every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must also update each index, and they consume storage. Index selectively — the frequently filtered/joined/sorted, high-selectivity columns — rather than every column. Over-indexing degrades a write-heavy system.",
    "index trade-off · reads faster / writes slower · storage cost · selectivity · index selectively")

add_solved_problem("S35 · Hands-on","Choose and create the right index for a slow query",
    "A grades page runs SELECT * FROM Enrollment WHERE roll = ? for a student's transcript, and it's slow. Diagnose and fix it.",
    ["Confirm the filter column: the query filters (and joins) on roll — the hot column.",
     "Check the current plan: EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM Enrollment WHERE roll=101; — a full scan means no useful index.",
     "Create a targeted index: CREATE INDEX idx_enrollment_roll ON Enrollment(roll);",
     "Re-run EXPLAIN — it should now show an index lookup instead of a scan.",
     "Don't over-do it: add indexes only for the columns actually filtered/joined (roll, course_id), not every column."],
    "EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM Enrollment WHERE roll=101;   -- shows a full scan\nCREATE INDEX idx_enrollment_roll ON Enrollment(roll);   -- now a fast B-tree lookup",
    "~6 min. Show the EXPLAIN before/after if a live DB is available; otherwise describe scan→lookup. Reinforce selective indexing.")
