concept_apply("S36 · Concept 1 · [THEORY]","The SQL Map (Synthesis)",
    "A real backend query touches the whole map at once: a Daraz sales dashboard JOINs orders to customers (S33), filters with WHERE (S23), summarises with GROUP BY/HAVing (S26), handles NULLs (S27), reads from a VIEW (S30), and runs fast because of INDEXES (S35). One report, fifteen sessions.",
    "\"SQL clauses run in the order I type them.\" No — the engine runs FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY. That's why you can't use a SELECT alias in WHERE, and why aggregate filters go in HAVING, not WHERE.",
    "SQL is one coherent system: DDL builds structures, DML queries/modifies data, and views, joins, procedures, triggers, and indexes extend it. Within a query the logical execution order is FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY — different from the written order — which explains alias scope and why aggregate conditions belong in HAVING.",
    "SQL map · DDL/DML/views/joins/procedures/triggers/indexes · clause execution order · write vs run order")

add_solved_problem("S36 · Lab · EASY","Basic SELECT/WHERE and pattern matching",
    "(1) List name and program of all 4th-semester BIM students. (2) List students whose name starts with 'A'.",
    ["(1) Filter on two columns with AND: program='BIM' AND semester=4.",
     "(1) Project just the two requested columns: SELECT name, program.",
     "(2) Use LIKE with the % wildcard for 'starts with A': name LIKE 'A%'.",
     "(2) '%' matches any following characters; a leading 'A' fixes the first letter.",
     "Test each against the seed data (Anjali matches; Sita/Hari do not)."],
    "1) SELECT name, program FROM Student WHERE program='BIM' AND semester=4;\n2) SELECT name FROM Student WHERE name LIKE 'A%';",
    "~4 min. Warm-up: reinforce WHERE with AND and LIKE 'A%'.")
add_solved_problem("S36 · Lab · MEDIUM","Aggregation, top-N, and the 'missing' rows",
    "(3) Average marks per course, only courses with >30 students. (4) Students with NO enrollment. (5) Top 3 students by marks.",
    ["(3) GROUP BY course_id, then filter groups with HAVING COUNT(*)>30 (not WHERE).",
     "(4) 'No enrollment' = LEFT JOIN and keep NULL right side, or NOT EXISTS.",
     "(4) LEFT JOIN Enrollment ... WHERE e.roll IS NULL isolates the unmatched students.",
     "(5) ORDER BY marks DESC then LIMIT 3 for the top three.",
     "Note (3) uses HAVING (aggregate filter); (4) uses the S33/S29 'missing rows' pattern."],
    "3) SELECT course_id, AVG(marks) FROM Enrollment GROUP BY course_id HAVING COUNT(*)>30;\n4) SELECT s.name FROM Student s LEFT JOIN Enrollment e ON s.roll=e.roll WHERE e.roll IS NULL;\n5) SELECT s.name, e.marks FROM Student s JOIN Enrollment e ON s.roll=e.roll ORDER BY e.marks DESC LIMIT 3;",
    "~6 min. These three cover HAVING, the LEFT-JOIN/NULL trick, and ORDER BY+LIMIT.")
add_solved_problem("S36 · Lab · HARD","Subqueries, views, and 'for all'",
    "(6) Highest-paid instructor. (7) Create and query a MeritList view. (8) Students enrolled in EVERY IT course.",
    ["(6) Highest-paid = salary equals the MAX salary (handles ties): WHERE salary=(SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Instructor).",
     "(7) Save the join as a view: CREATE VIEW MeritList AS SELECT roll,name,marks FROM Student JOIN Enrollment USING(roll); then SELECT * FROM MeritList ORDER BY marks DESC.",
     "(8) 'Every IT course' = there is NO IT course the student is NOT enrolled in (double NOT EXISTS).",
     "(8) Outer: student s. Inner-1: IT courses c. Inner-2: NOT EXISTS enrollment of s in c.",
     "Recognise (8) as relational division — the S29 double-negation pattern."],
    "6) SELECT name FROM Instructor WHERE salary=(SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Instructor);\n8) SELECT s.name FROM Student s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Course c WHERE c.dept='IT' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Enrollment e WHERE e.roll=s.roll AND e.course_id=c.course_id));",
    "~7 min. The capstone: subquery MAX, a view, and the double-NOT-EXISTS 'for all'.")
add_table_slide("S36 · Concept 3 · comparison","Common-mistakes review — the classic Unit-5 bugs",
    ["Mistake","Why it's wrong","The fix","Session"],
    [["WHERE grade = NULL","= NULL is always UNKNOWN → no rows","WHERE grade IS NULL","S27"],
     ["UPDATE/DELETE with no WHERE","changes/wipes EVERY row","add WHERE; SELECT-first","S31"],
     ["WHERE COUNT(*) > 30","aggregates illegal in WHERE","use HAVING COUNT(*)>30","S26"],
     ["NATURAL JOIN everywhere","silently joins on all shared names","use ON / USING explicitly","S33"],
     ["NOT IN with a NULL in the set","returns no rows unexpectedly","filter NULLs / use NOT EXISTS","S28"],
     ["INNER when you needed LEFT","hides the unmatched rows you wanted","use LEFT OUTER JOIN","S33"],
     ["money as FLOAT","binary rounding drifts totals","use DECIMAL(p,s)","S22"]],
    per_page=7,widths=[2.3,2.4,2.2,0.8],fs=10,
    note="These seven bugs account for most real-world SQL errors. Recognising them on sight — and the one-line fix for each — is what separates a fluent SQL writer from a beginner.")
